{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790975573,"nonce":"CnCqPQqlCE6SllYa5AEUUXPUGkc=","title":"web_backend raises for every native the adapter did not write by hand, even ones that are one WebIDL call","message":"`web_backend` / `hashlink_web_backend` forward a native to `crate::web` when the adapter defines it, and otherwise emit a stub that raises \"`\u003cnative\u003e` is not available on the web\". x-idl already knows enough to do better for many of them. A trait tagged `#[idl(\"GPUTexture\")]` names its WebIDL interface, the wire has an encoder method for every operation and attribute, and convert.rs already turns records into the wire's dictionaries. A native that is one member of that interface could be generated, as long as its arguments and result convert.\n\nIn xgpu (2026-10-02), 24 of the 144 natives its web.rs lacks are that shape. Examples: GPUTexture's width/height/format/usage/... getters; GPUDevice's createSampler, createBindGroupLayout, createPipelineLayout, createQuerySet, createRenderBundleEncoder, push/popErrorScope, lost, and create*PipelineAsync; GPUQuerySet's count and destroy; GPURenderBundleEncoder.finish; GPUDeviceLostInfo's reason and message; GPUShaderModule.getCompilationInfo. Bundle encoder draws and GPUCompilationInfo's messages are close behind. Their names match through a mixin, or the result is a sequence the declaration reads as count and index. xgpu's git-bug 106cf53fe7b624d802a6e53bffdb0a23e9ef7da8d3e4163cb6a461cb047b05ac tracks the whole gap.\n\nWhat generation needs:\n\n- matching: a method maps to the trait's interface member by name, `create`/`get` + PascalCase name, or an explicit override on the method for a rename. An untagged trait (xgpu's GpuEncoder, which folds several WebGPU encoders into one) is never matched.\n- a contract with the adapter's hand-written module, which owns the handle tables and the mailbox. The generated forward needs to: check a receiver handle is live and of its kind; take the wire encoder; allocate a handle of a kind for an interface result and give its wire Handle; make a synchronous call with a reply buffer and decode the result (getters); make a call whose Promise settles a runtime Future (async creates, popErrorScope, lost); and release a handle (destroy). xgpu's web.rs has all of these as private helpers today (State's Slabs, `handle`, `transient`, `Reply`, `Promise`/`pending`, `flush`, `release`). The contract has to be small enough that xwindow's web.rs could adopt it too.\n- conversion of each argument and result type the declaration uses: numbers, bool, Text, Buffer, Enum\u003cT\u003e (an IDL enum by index), records (convert.rs), interface handles, Option, and Future\u003cT\u003e for Promise\u003cT\u003e.\n\nA hand-written function of the same name still wins, so an adapter can override any generated one.","files":null}]}